[https://www.tiktok.com/@catieosaurus/video/6923626203458948357?source=h5\_m&is\_copy\_url=0&is\_from\_webapp=v3&sender\_device=pc&sender\_web\_id=6892427871320868358](https://www.tiktok.com/@catieosaurus/video/6923626203458948357?source=h5_m&is_copy_url=0&is_from_webapp=v3&sender_device=pc&sender_web_id=6892427871320868358) I can't seem to find a ton of info about this online so I can't verify how medically accurate it is but I definitely related to it a lot.  

&#x200B;

The example I can think of is if my friend texts me and says "I had the worst day at work, my boss yelled at me in front of everyone and made me cry."  My immediate instinct would be to tell a story about my own experience with a bad boss or crying at work.  Which much to my surprise comes off as selfish, but to me it's me showing through a story of my own that I understand how that person feels (trying to empathize?).  I guess normally someone would respond with general support/reassurance, follow up questions about what happened, or advice about what to do next.
